<!-- RoT DTD GOAL trust contract (1.0.0)
     SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-or-later OR EUPL-1.2
     Copyright 2026 Saimonokuma.

     A DTD-style declaration of the ONE thing this engine cannot afford to be
     ambiguous about: WHO IS SPEAKING. Every string below is a verdict -- a
     sentence only RoT DTD GOAL itself may utter. Everything a criterion, a hook
     payload or any other command prints is data, and data goes inside a fence.

     This file is not parsed by an XML processor. It is a machine-checked
     contract: `goal.sh contract --verify` (and, in the suite,
     test_untrusted_output_cannot_forge_a_verdict) enforces BOTH directions --

       1. every VERDICT declared here is really emitted by a script, so the
          contract cannot fill up with vocabulary nobody uses;
       2. every declared VERDICT is provably quarantined when it arrives from
          an untrusted channel, so a criterion printing "GOAL COMPLETE" cannot
          put that sentence into the reader's context in the engine's voice.

     Direction 1 is what keeps this file honest as the engine grows: add a new
     banner to stop_gate.sh and forget to declare it, and the contract check
     fails with the banner named. A contract nobody can violate is decoration.
-->
<!DOCTYPE rot-dtd-goal-trust [

  <!-- ================= WHO MAY SPEAK ================= -->
  <!ELEMENT verdict    (#PCDATA)>   <!-- source: scripts/stop_gate.sh, scripts/goal.sh -->
  <!ELEMENT untrusted  (CDATA)>     <!-- source: any verify command, any hook payload -->
  <!ATTLIST untrusted  fence CDATA #FIXED "<![GF-UNTRUSTED[ ... ]]>">

  <!-- ================= THE VERDICT VOCABULARY =================
       Each entity is a literal string. If it appears at the start of a line in
       the engine's own voice, RoT DTD GOAL said it. If it appears inside a
       fence, something else did, and it means nothing.
  -->
  <!ENTITY VERDICT.complete     "GOAL COMPLETE">
  <!ENTITY VERDICT.refused      "COMPLETION REFUSED">
  <!ENTITY VERDICT.ledger       "LEDGER OK">
  <!ENTITY VERDICT.drift        "INTEGRITY DRIFT">
  <!ENTITY VERDICT.escalate     "ESCALATE">
  <!ENTITY VERDICT.flaky        "FLAKY WARNING">
  <!ENTITY VERDICT.redteam      "RED TEAM WARNING">
  <!ENTITY VERDICT.mutation     "MUTATION WARNING">
  <!ENTITY VERDICT.attestation  "ATTESTATION OK">
  <!-- 4.0.0: the contract-auditor's bench finding -- these seven were spoken
       by the engine but never declared, so the forgery attack never covered
       them. Declared now; the attack covers every one automatically. -->
  <!ENTITY VERDICT.ledgerdrift  "LEDGER DRIFT">
  <!ENTITY VERDICT.attestfail   "ATTESTATION FAILED">
  <!ENTITY VERDICT.stall        "STALL DETECTED">
  <!ENTITY VERDICT.contractok   "CONTRACT OK">
  <!ENTITY VERDICT.contractbad  "CONTRACT DRIFT">
  <!ENTITY VERDICT.schemaok     "SCHEMA OK">
  <!ENTITY VERDICT.schemabad    "SCHEMA DRIFT">
  <!-- 4.0.0: emitted when the gate's own time budget expires mid-pipeline --
       a slow gate must cost an iteration, never a verdict (LAW.18). -->
  <!ENTITY VERDICT.incomplete   "VERIFICATION INCOMPLETE">

  <!-- ================= THE QUEUE SPEC GRAMMAR =================
       A queued goal is a two-verb TSV file. The grammar is DECLARED here and
       READ by scripts/goal.sh:gf_spec_validate -- it is not hardcoded in the
       validator. Edit the content model below and the validator's behaviour
       changes with it; test_many_goals_run_in_order proves that binding by
       narrowing the model and watching a previously-legal spec be refused.

       Cardinality is the usual one: a bare name means exactly one row, `+`
       means one or more, `*` means any number.
  -->
  <!ELEMENT spec  (GOAL, CRIT+)>
  <!ELEMENT GOAL  (#PCDATA)>   <!-- goal text <TAB> init options            -->
  <!ELEMENT CRIT  (#PCDATA)>   <!-- id <TAB> description <TAB> verify command [<TAB> deps ;-joined globs, since 4.0.0] -->

  <!-- ================= THE INSTRUCTION CHANNEL =================
       When the queue advances, the gate is no longer reporting a result: it is
       issuing an instruction to whoever is working the loop. That is a
       different speech act from a verdict and it is tagged as one, so an agent
       reading the transcript can tell "this is what happened" from "this is
       what to do next" without inferring it from prose.
  -->
  <!ELEMENT gf:instruction (#PCDATA)>
  <!ATTLIST gf:instruction
            goal  CDATA #REQUIRED
            step  CDATA #REQUIRED>

  <!-- ================= THE IMMUTABLE LAWS =================
       Stated as declarations because they are not preferences. Each is
       enforced by a named case in tests/run_tests.sh; INVARIANTS.tsv maps
       every one of them to the test that can falsify it.
  -->
  <!ENTITY LAW.1  "Done is proven, not promised -- completion is computed from exit codes, never from text.">
  <!ENTITY LAW.2  "The checks are checked -- seals, negative controls, mutation.">
  <!ENTITY LAW.3  "Feedback is targeted, never a blind re-feed.">
  <!ENTITY LAW.4  "Every loop has an exit -- budget, stall, human.">
  <!ENTITY LAW.5  "State stays inspectable -- a human with cat must understand it.">
  <!ENTITY LAW.6  "The floor stays bash + coreutils.">
  <!ENTITY LAW.7  "Instruments must be able to fail, survive hostile ground, and never lie in the reassuring direction.">
  <!ENTITY LAW.8  "Every measurement is consumed, and every classification re-earns its place.">
  <!ENTITY LAW.9  "A pass is evidence only if reproducible.">
  <!ENTITY LAW.10 "Nothing on the label may be typed -- counts and claims are generated or bounded.">
  <!ENTITY LAW.11 "No decision is made from a clock -- ordering comes from a counter that only counts up.">
  <!ENTITY LAW.12 "Untrusted output is fenced; the engine's voice is not forgeable by the code it runs.">
  <!ENTITY LAW.13 "A queued goal starts only after its predecessor was verified, never on a promise.">
  <!ENTITY LAW.14 "A grammar is declared once and read by the code that enforces it.">
  <!ENTITY LAW.15 "An on-disk record is append-only: field numbers are dense, never reused, never reordered.">
  <!ENTITY LAW.16 "An agent may only speak in a declared element, and every declared agent exists.">
  <!ENTITY LAW.17 "A refusal always carries a task -- the gate blocks with the next step, never with a wall.">
  <!ENTITY LAW.18 "A gate that runs out of time blocks and says so -- a slow pipeline costs an iteration, never a verdict.">

  <!-- ================= THE RECORD SCHEMA =================
       WHY THIS EXISTS, and it is the most-earned section in this file.

       The recurring defect of this project has not been logic. It has been
       COLUMN DRIFT. The ledger grew a fifth field (seal generation); the
       timings file grew a sixth (gen); each time, code that read `$4` or
       assumed "four columns" kept running and quietly meant something else,
       and a test that asserted "a ledger row carries four columns" turned
       from a specification into an expired snapshot that had to be deleted.

       Protobuf solved this decades ago and the solution is not a serialiser:
       it is a DISCIPLINE. Fields are NUMBERED, numbers are NEVER reused, and
       new fields are only ever APPENDED -- so an old reader and a new writer
       still agree about what field 3 means. That discipline is what is
       declared here, in DTD form, over plain TSV files a human can still cat.

       Each field is `<number>=<name>:<content-model>@<since>`:
         PCDATA -- structured text the engine parses (ids, integers, statuses)
         CDATA  -- raw text that may contain anything, never interpreted as
                   markup or instruction (descriptions, commands, goal text)
         @since -- the version the field first appeared in

       `goal.sh schema --verify` enforces, mechanically:
         1. numbers are dense from 1, with no gaps and no duplicates;
         2. `since` never DECREASES as the number grows -- which is exactly
            "append-only", because inserting a field in the middle or
            renumbering an old one makes an older version appear later;
         3. a live goal directory's files carry exactly as many columns as
            the highest declared field number.

       Rule 2 is the one that would have caught every drift this project had.
  -->
  <!ELEMENT records (record+)>
  <!ELEMENT record  (field+)>
  <!ATTLIST record  name CDATA #REQUIRED  file CDATA #REQUIRED>
  <!ELEMENT field   (#PCDATA)>

  <!-- Each record is ALSO declared the ordinary DTD way -- a sequence content
       model, exactly like <!ELEMENT note (to,from,heading,body)>. That is not
       duplication for its own sake: it is a SECOND, INDEPENDENT statement of
       the same fact, written in a different shape.

       `goal.sh schema --verify` cross-checks the two. The sequence below must
       name exactly the fields of the numbered declaration, in exactly the
       numbered order. A typo in either one is then caught by the other, which
       is the only reason a redundant declaration earns its place. Where they
       disagree, neither is trusted and the check fails naming the record.

       Protobuf-style numbering answers "which field is this, forever".
       DTD-style sequence answers "what does a whole record look like".
       Both are true here, and the tree refuses to let them drift apart. -->
  <!ELEMENT ledger     (id, hash, sealed_epoch, sealed_iso, seal_gen)>
  <!ELEMENT timings    (ts, id, allowed, duration, outcome, gen)>
  <!ELEMENT queue      (name, status, after, spec)>
  <!ELEMENT consumers  (event, class, consumer, effect, why_not_decisional)>
  <!ELEMENT invariants (law, statement, enforcing_case, breaking_looks_like)>
  <!ELEMENT history    (ts, outcome, iteration, max_iterations, total, passed, duration_s, goal)>

  <!ENTITY RECORD.ledger
    "ledger|.claude/goal/ledger|1=id:PCDATA@v2|2=hash:PCDATA@v2|3=sealed_epoch:PCDATA@v2|4=sealed_iso:PCDATA@v2|5=seal_gen:PCDATA@1.0.0">

  <!ENTITY RECORD.timings
    "timings|.claude/goal/timings.tsv|1=ts:PCDATA@v3.3|2=id:PCDATA@v3.3|3=allowed:PCDATA@v3.3|4=duration:PCDATA@v3.3|5=outcome:PCDATA@v3.3|6=gen:PCDATA@1.0.0">

  <!ENTITY RECORD.queue
    "queue|.claude/goal/queue.tsv|1=name:PCDATA@1.0.0|2=status:PCDATA@1.0.0|3=after:PCDATA@1.0.0|4=spec:CDATA@1.0.0">

  <!ENTITY RECORD.consumers
    "consumers|hooks/event_consumers.tsv|1=event:PCDATA@v3.4|2=class:PCDATA@v3.4|3=consumer:PCDATA@v3.4|4=effect:CDATA@v3.4|5=why_not_decisional:CDATA@1.0.0">

  <!ENTITY RECORD.invariants
    "invariants|hooks/INVARIANTS.tsv|1=law:PCDATA@1.0.0|2=statement:CDATA@1.0.0|3=enforcing_case:PCDATA@1.0.0|4=breaking_looks_like:CDATA@1.0.0">

  <!ENTITY RECORD.history
    "history|.claude/goal/history.tsv|1=ts:PCDATA@1.0.0|2=outcome:PCDATA@1.0.0|3=iteration:PCDATA@1.0.0|4=max_iterations:PCDATA@1.0.0|5=total:PCDATA@1.0.0|6=passed:PCDATA@1.0.0|7=duration_s:PCDATA@1.0.0|8=goal:CDATA@1.0.0">

  <!-- ================= THE POLICY ATTRIBUTES =================
       A parameter entity, because the same enumeration is used three times and
       a copied enumeration is a future disagreement.
  -->
  <!ENTITY % policy "(off|warn|strict)">

  <!-- The gate's three policies, declared with their ENUMERATION and their
       DEFAULT -- the ATTLIST form, used for exactly what it is for.

       This is not documentation. `goal.sh contract --verify` checks that the
       default declared here is the default `cmd_init` actually writes into
       state, and that `cmd_set` accepts exactly these values and no others. A
       README that says "default warn" while the code writes "off" is the
       classic drift; here the two are bound and the build fails on drift.

       GATE_FLAKY's default is `strict` because it was MEASURED
       (tests/experiments/flaky_policy.sh: 0 false refusals in 40 goals), not
       because strict sounds safer. GATE_MUTATE's default is `off` because it
       damages a copy of your files to prove a check can fail, and that is a
       cost a user must opt into.
  -->
  <!ELEMENT gate EMPTY>
  <!ATTLIST gate
            redteam  %policy;  "warn"
            mutate   %policy;  "off"
            flaky    %policy;  "strict">

  <!-- ================= THE UNPARSED CHANNELS =================
       NOTATION is the DTD construct for data the processor must NOT parse: it
       says "here is the format, here is how to handle it", and nothing more.
       That is precisely the boundary this engine cares about most.

       Every stream below enters the engine from outside its own voice. Each is
       declared with the notation that says how it must be handled, and each
       untrusted one must provably pass through gf_quarantine before a reader
       ever sees it -- checked by `contract --verify`, so a THIRD call site
       added later cannot quietly skip the fence.

       The last entry is a soft edge the fourth review named, and it is
       declared here rather than left in a review note: the verify command TEXT
       is authored by a planner, not by the engine, and it is rendered without
       a fence (never at line start). It is trusted by PROVENANCE, not by
       inspection. Writing that down is the difference between a known boundary
       and an unexamined one.
  -->
  <!NOTATION untrusted-text  SYSTEM "text/plain; must-be-fenced">
  <!NOTATION parsed-tsv      SYSTEM "text/tab-separated-values; validated-before-use">
  <!NOTATION append-only-log SYSTEM "text/plain; never-rewritten">
  <!NOTATION trusted-by-provenance SYSTEM "text/plain; author=planner; not-fenced">

  <!ENTITY verify-output SYSTEM ".claude/goal/out/ID.log"   NDATA untrusted-text>
  <!ENTITY hook-payload  SYSTEM "hook-stdin"                  NDATA untrusted-text>
  <!ENTITY queue-spec    SYSTEM "specs/NAME.tsv"             NDATA parsed-tsv>
  <!ENTITY journal       SYSTEM ".claude/goal/journal.log"   NDATA append-only-log>
  <!ENTITY verify-text   SYSTEM ".claude/goal/criteria/ID"   NDATA trusted-by-provenance>

  <!-- ================= THE AGENT ROSTER =================
       An agent is a sub-model this engine may summon. It is the one component
       whose output is PROSE, which makes it the one component that could
       smuggle a verdict into the reader's context by sounding official.

       So each agent is declared with the ELEMENT it is permitted to speak in,
       and nothing it produces is a decision -- the gate still decides by exit
       code, always. `goal.sh contract --verify` checks BOTH directions: every
       declared agent has a file, and every file in agents/ is declared. A
       roster nobody checks is a directory listing.

       Format: `name|element|content|what it may never do`
  -->
  <!ELEMENT gf:spec      (CDATA)>    <!-- planner: a queue spec, validated before use -->
  <!ELEMENT gf:criterion (CDATA)>    <!-- verifier: a proposed criterion command      -->
  <!ELEMENT gf:strategy  (#PCDATA)>  <!-- critic: a different approach, not a verdict -->
  <!ELEMENT gf:attack    (CDATA)>    <!-- red team: how to make a check fail          -->
  <!ELEMENT gf:finding   (#PCDATA)>  <!-- forensic: what the record shows             -->
  <!ELEMENT gf:queue     (CDATA)>    <!-- architect: rows for `queue add`             -->
  <!ELEMENT gf:audit     (#PCDATA)>  <!-- contract auditor: undeclared strings found  -->

  <!ENTITY AGENT.1 "goal-planner|gf:spec|a queue spec in the declared TSV grammar|may never mark a criterion passed">
  <!ENTITY AGENT.2 "goal-verifier|gf:criterion|a verify command and its --deps|may never run the gate or edit the ledger">
  <!ENTITY AGENT.3 "goal-critic|gf:strategy|two or three genuinely different approaches|may never declare the goal complete">
  <!ENTITY AGENT.4 "goal-redteam|gf:attack|the input that makes a criterion fail|may never weaken a criterion to make it pass">
  <!ENTITY AGENT.5 "goal-forensic|gf:finding|what the journal, ledger and timings actually show|may never infer completion from a pattern">
  <!ENTITY AGENT.6 "goal-queue-architect|gf:queue|a dependency-ordered decomposition|may never create a forward dependency">
  <!ENTITY AGENT.7 "goal-contract-auditor|gf:audit|engine strings that are not declared here|may never add a verdict without a test">

]>
